    <div style="margin-top:28px;" class="prose">
      <p><strong>1970s — The Era of Price Shocks:</strong> The decade opened with oil at $1.21/bbl — a price so low it reflected a market governed by long-term contracts and producer concessions rather than competitive dynamics. The 1973 Arab oil embargo triggered a quadrupling of prices from $4.10 to $13.00/bbl in a single month (January 1974), while the 1979 Iranian Revolution drove prices from $14.50 to over $40/bbl. Average prices for the decade were $9.70/bbl, heavily skewed by the two shock events, with a standard deviation of $8.85 — a coefficient of variation of 91%, the highest of any decade.</p>

      <p><strong>1980s — The Great Deflation:</strong> After peaking at $40.30 in late 1980, prices entered a sustained decline as energy conservation, non-OPEC supply expansion, and Saudi Arabia's 1986 decision to flood the market pushed prices below $10/bbl. The decade averaged $25.55/bbl, but the range ($9.62–$40.30) tells the story of a market in structural transition from OPEC-administered pricing to market-determined pricing.</p>

      <p><strong>1990s — The Low-Price Equilibrium:</strong> The cheapest decade in real terms, the 1990s averaged just $18.19/bbl with the lowest standard deviation ($3.82) of any period. The Gulf War caused a brief spike to $34.50 in October 1990, but prices quickly reverted. The Asian financial crisis drove prices to a decade low of $10.41 in December 1998, a level not seen since 1974 in nominal terms.</p>

      <p><strong>2000s — The Super-Cycle:</strong> Demand from emerging economies — particularly China — combined with years of underinvestment in upstream capacity to drive the most dramatic price increase in oil market history. Prices rose from $25/bbl in 2002 to $132.83/bbl in July 2008, a 430% increase. The subsequent collapse to $41.34 by December 2008 was equally violent. The decade recorded the widest range ($18.52–$132.83) and highest standard deviation ($25.54).</p>

      <p><strong>2010s — Elevated but Unstable:</strong> The 2010s hold the record for highest average price at $76.45/bbl, driven initially by the post-crisis recovery and the Arab Spring, then undermined by the US shale revolution which triggered the 2014–2016 price collapse from $108 to $29.78/bbl. Average month-over-month volatility of 5.66% reflects persistent tension between OPEC+ supply management and growing US output.</p>

      <p><strong>2020s — Pandemic, War, and Transition:</strong> The current decade has already seen the most extreme short-term volatility on record. The COVID-19 demand shock drove prices from $61.63 to $21.04/bbl in just three months (January–April 2020), while the Russia-Ukraine conflict pushed them from $78.07 to $116.80/bbl in the first half of 2022. Average MoM volatility of 8.16% is the highest of any decade, reflecting a market increasingly driven by geopolitical risk and demand uncertainty around the energy transition.</p>
